DNS differs between clients|Some clients reach an old address after a DNS change.|dig example.com A|Compare authoritative answers, recursive answers and TTLs from affected and unaffected clients. Check local hosts entries and application caches separately.|Correct an authoritative mistake if present; otherwise allow documented cache lifetimes or refresh a specific managed cache. Do not keep changing the record while valid old answers remain cached.|Confirm affected clients obtain the intended address and successful requests after the old TTL window.
TCP connection times out|A client repeatedly sends connection attempts without receiving a response.|tcpdump -ni eth0 -c 50 'host 192.0.2.10 and tcp port 443'|Capture at appropriate authorized points on both ends. Determine whether SYN arrives, a SYN-ACK leaves and the return path reaches the client.|Correct the evidenced route, listener or narrow firewall rule. If replies take another path, examine stateful filtering and source selection.|Observe a completed handshake and a successful application request, not merely a passing ping.
Connection refused|A service address responds with an immediate TCP reset.|ss -lntp|Check the destination host's listener, port and bind address; confirm the client reaches the intended host. A reset suggests active rejection rather than silent packet loss.|Start or correct the intended service, fix port mapping or correct an explicit rejection policy after confirming ownership.|Connect from the original client and verify the expected application identity and response.
TLS hostname failure|HTTPS connects but certificate verification reports a name mismatch.|openssl s_client -connect example.com:443 -servername example.com -verify_return_error -verify_hostname example.com|Inspect the certificate names, chain and selected virtual host. Confirm DNS points to the intended endpoint and SNI uses the requested hostname.|Install the correct certificate or fix routing/SNI configuration. Keep verification enabled; bypassing it does not repair identity.|Repeat verification with the real client trust store and hostname, then test application traffic.
Large transfers stall|Small health checks pass but larger HTTPS responses repeatedly hang.|ip link show|Compare effective MTU along tunnels and inspect retransmissions and ICMP packet-too-big feedback. Distinguish a path-MTU black hole from server-side buffering.|Correct tunnel MTU or permit required ICMP feedback using the network's policy. Apply MSS adjustment only when justified by the path design.|Test representative payload sizes in both directions and confirm retransmissions and stalls disappear.
Idle pooled connections fail|The first request after idle time fails, while an immediate retry succeeds.|ss -o state established|Compare application pool idle lifetime with proxy, load-balancer and NAT idle timeouts. Check whether the client reuses a connection already expired by an intermediary.|Expire or validate pooled connections before the shortest relevant idle timeout and bound retry behavior for safe operations.|Repeat a controlled idle interval and confirm the first request succeeds without a retry burst.
Retry-amplified outage|A dependency slows and incoming requests multiply despite steady user traffic.|ss -s|Compare attempt counts at each layer with original request volume and dependency saturation. Identify synchronized retries and missing overall deadlines.|Cap retries, add backoff with jitter and shed excess work. Restore dependency headroom before gradually returning normal traffic.|Confirm attempts per user request, queue age and error rate return to expected levels.
Wrong client address in logs|Every request appears to come from a proxy, or clients can spoof recorded IP addresses.|curl -I https://example.com|Inspect the trusted proxy chain and how forwarding headers are stripped, added and consumed. Do not trust arbitrary incoming X-Forwarded-For values.|Configure edge proxies to sanitize headers and applications to trust only known proxy hops. Preserve the actual transport peer for audit.|Test normal and deliberately supplied forwarding headers and confirm the recorded identity follows the trusted chain.
Intermittent backend failures|Only some requests through a load balancer fail.|curl -I --max-time 10 https://example.com|Correlate failures with backend identity, zone and version. Compare health-check paths with the failing business operation and inspect session affinity.|Remove or repair the evidenced unhealthy cohort while retaining capacity. Correct health checks if they pass on broken serving paths.|Verify every intended backend passes representative requests and aggregate errors recover across zones.
Outbound connections exhausted|New outbound connections fail during traffic spikes while established ones continue.|ss -tan state time-wait|Compare connection churn, source-port usage and NAT allocation metrics. Many TIME_WAIT entries alone are not proof of exhaustion; confirm allocation failures.|Reuse connections appropriately, bound concurrency and expand the constrained egress capacity if justified. Avoid arbitrary TCP timeout reductions.|Load-test within approved limits and verify new connections succeed with stable port and NAT headroom.
